    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Gaelic kingdom straddling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, with lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, ruled from the rock fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>’s monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, founded under its kings in 563, carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and learning to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and Northumbria.</li><li>Weakened by defeats and Pictish attack, it merged with Pictland in the ninth century, and its Gaelic speech became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>’s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> bears a carved human footprint, a rock basin and a boar, which are thought to have been used in the inauguration of kings.</p></aside>
    </section>

