    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunadd"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunadd.jpg" alt="Dunadd" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunadd_Fort_20080427.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otter, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dál Riata was a Gaelic kingdom that straddled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, with lands in the north-east of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a> and, across the sea, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a>. Its name belonged originally to a population group in Antrim, and by around 500 its rulers held territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as well. Irish annals and later Scottish tradition record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergus-mor">Fergus Mór mac Eirc</a> ‘took part of Britain’ with the people of Dál Riata and died there, an event entered under about 501, and medieval writers presented the kingdom as the product of a migration from Ireland. Many historians and archaeologists now doubt that a large-scale movement took place, noting that Argyll and Antrim had long been linked by the sea and that Gaelic speech may already have been established in Argyll; the Fergus story was shaped by later dynasties seeking a founding hero. What is clear is that by the sixth century a Gaelic-speaking kingdom existed on both sides of the channel, its British part a mountainous coastland of sea lochs and islands where travel by boat was easier than by land. Its chief centre became the fortified rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a>, rising from the bog of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moine-mhor">Moine Mhor</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilmartin">Kilmartin</a>, while in Ireland its lands lay along the north Antrim coast.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/columba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columba.jpg" alt="Columba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columba_at_Bridei%27s_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. R. Skelton (Joseph Ratcliffe Skelton; 1865–1927) (illustrator), erroneously credited as John R. Skelton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was a land of small farms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and the sea. Most people lived in scattered homesteads, raising cattle, sheep and pigs and growing oats and barley on the limited arable ground, and wealth and status were measured above all in cattle. Society was arranged in kindreds, and the free population owed tribute and service to their lords and kings. The <em>Senchus fer nAlban</em>, a text whose core probably dates from the seventh century, lists the households of the main kindreds and their obligations to provide oarsmen for sea levies, a rare glimpse of the resources of an early medieval kingdom. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> have revealed workshops for fine metalwork, including moulds for brooches, together with pottery and glass imported from the Continent, showing that luxury goods reached the kingdom along Atlantic sea routes. The rock bears carvings of a human footprint, a basin, a boar and an ogham inscription. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> shaped the kingdom deeply. In 563 the Irish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> founded a monastery on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-columba">Life of Columba</a></em> his community kept close ties with the kings of Dál Riata. Iona grew into the head of a family of monasteries in Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and a centre of learning, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> production and sculpture, including its great high crosses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain.jpg" alt="Áedán mac Gabráin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalriada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dál Riata fought on land and sea. Its kings commanded war bands of noble warriors and levies of free men, and the <em>Senchus fer nAlban</em> shows that military obligation was reckoned in boats and crews as well as men, reflecting a kingdom whose communities were linked by water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a>, who according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> was ordained king by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>, campaigned widely: the annals record his expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> around 580 and a battle around 582 in Manann, either the Isle of Man or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manaw-gododdin">Manaw</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a>, and Adomnán describes his battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeatae">Miathi</a>. In 603, however, his large army was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-degsastan">Battle of Degsastan</a>, after which, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> wrote, no king of the Scots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> dared to make war on the English. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congal-caech">Congal Cáech</a> of the Ulaid in his war against the Irish high king and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-moira">Battle of Mag Rath</a> in 637, which weakened Dál Riata’s position in Ireland; he was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Britons of Strathclyde</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-strathcarron">Strathcarron</a> in 642. Rivalry between the kindreds led to internal warfare, including a sea battle in 719 between Cenél nGabráin and Cenél Loairn, among the earliest naval battles recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. In the 730s and 740s the Pictish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a> attacked Dál Riata, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> in 736.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/islay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islay.jpg" alt="Islay" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Ellen,_Islay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J M Briscoe, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dál Riata was less a unified state than a federation of kindreds, each ruled by its own leading family, under an over-king drawn from one of them. The <em>Senchus fer nAlban</em> names three main kindreds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>: Cenél nGabráin, centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a>; Cenél Loairn, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorn">Lorn</a> to the north; and Cenél nÓengusa on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-comgaill">Cenél Comgaill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowal">Cowal</a> appears in the annals as a further group. The kingship was most often held by Cenél nGabráin, the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a>, but from the late seventh century Cenél Loairn kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferchar-fota">Ferchar Fota</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selbach-mac-ferchair">Selbach</a> challenged and sometimes held it. Kings ruled through the personal loyalty of their kin and clients, collecting tribute in food and service and calling out levies for war. Law and custom followed Irish models, and the kingdom’s Irish lands tied its rulers into the politics of the northern Irish over-kingships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> records the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-druim-cett">Convention of Druim Cett</a>, at which Áedán mac Gabráin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aed-mac-ainmuirech">Áed mac Ainmuirech</a> met in the presence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>, around 575 or perhaps later; its terms are uncertain, but they appear to have settled the obligations of the Irish part of Dál Riata. Inauguration of kings probably took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a>, where the carved footprint may have played a part in the ceremony.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cain-adomnain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cain-adomnain.jpg" alt="Cáin Adomnáin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cain-adomnain">Cáin Adomnáin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regal_Guarantors_of_Cain_Admomnain.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SKIBLY101, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dál Riata’s greatest legacy was the spread of Gaelic language and culture across what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. When its royal and noble families merged with the Pictish elite in the ninth century, it was Gaelic, not Pictish, that became the language of the new kingdom of Alba, and the name Scotland itself derives from <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scoti">Scoti</a></em>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a>. The community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, founded under the kingdom’s protection, carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and to the English: in 635 the Iona monk Aidan founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> at the invitation of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, who had spent his exile among the Gaels, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> trained in the Iona tradition led the Northumbrian church until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-whitby">Synod of Whitby</a> in 664. Iona’s abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-columba">Life of Columba</a></em> around 700 and in 697 promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cain-adomnain">Law of the Innocents</a>, which sought to protect women, children and clergy in war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a>, a masterpiece of insular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> art, was probably made at least in part on Iona around 800. Later kings of Scots traced their descent through the Cenél nGabráin line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergus-mor">Fergus Mór</a>, and medieval tradition held that many early Scottish kings were buried on Iona. The Gaelic culture of the western Highlands and islands has its roots in the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts.jpg" alt="Óengus I of the Picts" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus I of the Picts</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%93engus_detail_sarcophagus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Son of Groucho of Scotland on Flikr., CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dál Riata lost its Irish lands and then its independence. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-moira">Mag Rath</a> in 637 its kings appear to have lost much of their influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a>, and the Irish part of the kingdom faded from the record, while the British part was weakened by rivalry between Cenél nGabráin and Cenél Loairn. In 736 the Pictish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a> invaded, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> and seized two sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selbach-mac-ferchair">Selbach</a>, and in 741 the annals record the ‘smiting’ of Dál Riata by Óengus. For several decades the kingdom seems to have been under Pictish overlordship, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aed-find">Áed Find</a>, who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in 768 and died in 778, restored a measure of independence. From 795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> was attacked repeatedly; in 806 sixty-eight members of the community were killed, and a new monastery was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> in Ireland. In the early ninth century kings of the Pictish line of Fergus, such as Constantín son of Fergus, appear to have ruled Dál Riata as well as Pictland. The final union came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Cináed mac Ailpín</a>, Kenneth MacAlpin, who became king of the Picts around 842 and was later remembered as a king of Dál Riata, although his origins are debated. Around 849 relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a>, and by about 900 the merged kingdom was called Alba.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Dál Riata was the Gaelic sea-kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> whose kings sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>’s monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, centre of the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and Northumbria, and whose merger with Pictland in the ninth century made Gaelic the language of the kingdom of Alba, the core of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</em></p>

